  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nLittle overall change to the forecast. Mid-level ridging will remain \nthe dominant feature across South Texas through much of the week, \nsupporting a continuation of hot temperatures and generally rain-\nfree conditions through Wednesday. Afternoon highs will range mid \n90s to around 105 degrees across portions of the Brush Country and \nRio Grande Plains. Despite some afternoon mixing, heat indices are \nstill expected to peak between 105-110 degrees each day, resulting \nin a continued moderate to locally major Heat Risk. Heat Advisories \nmay be needed this week if forecast trends continue. \n\nForecast confidence decreases late in the week as Tropical Storm \nBertha moves westward across the northern Gulf. Although the latest \nforecast keeps the center near the northern Gulf Coast with gradual \nweakening expected as it approaches the LA/TX border, uncertainty \nremains regarding both the exact track and the westward extent of \ntropical moisture. At this time, the greatest impacts are expected \nto remain east of South Texas, but low rain chances have been \nmaintained from the coast inland across the Coastal Plains late this \nweek into the weekend to account for the possibility of outer rain \nbands or deeper tropical moisture extending farther west than \nindicated by the current forecast. \n\nRegardless of the cyclone's exact track, increasing easterly swell \nwill likely reach the Middle Texas Coast beginning late this week, \nresulting in a possible increase of marine hazards and a heightened \nrisk of dangerous rip currents. Interests along the Texas Coast \nshould continue monitoring the latest forecast as confidence in the \nsystem's evolution is expected to improve over the coming days. \n\nAir temperatures are expected to remain above seasonal normals \nthrough early next week, with hot afternoons in the mid 90s to \naround 105, warm overnight lows in the mid 70s to lower 80s, and \nonly isolated to scattered showers possible if tropical moisture \nreaches the region.",
